Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A world that is dominated by China would be completely different from the current world which is dominated by the US. This is due to cultural and economic differences between the two powers.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note predicts how a world dominated by China would look, compared to a world dominated by the US.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>First, it is unlikely that a lack of demand from the US is going to cause a huge problem for China, since contrary to popular belief, China is not a export-based economy.  <a href=\"https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NE.EXP.GNFS.ZS?locations=CN-FR-DE-KR-US\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bank</a></li><li>China surpasses the US in state capacity, but the state is not always the bets at being able to use the resources that it has in the most efficient manner.<ul><li>This means that while nationally, China might dominate, they won't necessarily live better than the citizens of the US have.</li></ul></li><li>China is more selfish than the US, and would not provide the same public goods that the US as provided, though they are perfectly capable of doing so if needed.<ul><li>China's had a generally inward looking foreign policy in its history.</li></ul></li><li>Chinese culture won't be as influential as the US's was to the isolation of China was the rest of the world digitally.</li><li>Due to the weakness of IP protection and \"other institutional factors,\" innovation in China will likely be a lot of incremental improvements rather than big technological change.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a><ul><li>Although China might dominate, its dominance could be quite different from the dominance of the US.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>See above note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>This could be a reason why China has a different approach when it comes to being the dominant global power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies\">Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies</a><ul><li>This could potentially make it more likely that China will come to dominate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>This note echoes this sentiment.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon\">States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon</a><ul><li>With the rise of China, states around it will have to make this choice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>This could have been part of the reason for the rise of China.</li></ul></li></ul>","Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are two opposite routes to the same effect: one can show that ordinary life is already strange, or one can construct a world whose strangeness is manifest, and both leave the reader unable to take the familiar for granted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two routes are not equivalent in what they cost. The second requires machinery that the first does without, which bears on how much of the effect is attributable to the insight and how much to the construction.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Camus takes the first route, writing about the tragedy of human existence in the absence of transcendence and finding the absurd in mundane things.</li><li>Kafka takes the second, revealing worlds full of what cannot be understood, which represent transcendence that is impossible rather than merely absent.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>On this criterion Camus is the greater writer, since he does not rely on constructed scenarios to exhibit the absurdity of existence. The obvious objection is that the constructed scenario is doing a different job, namely isolating the structure from the noise of ordinary life, in which case the comparison is unfair.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome\">Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome</a><ul><li>The account of the absurd that both routes are trying to exhibit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a><ul><li>The response that follows from the first route.</li></ul></li></ul>","An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An act is compulsory (otherwise known as \"involuntary\") when the cause of the action is external, i.e., what is compelling the agent to conduct the action does not originate in the agent. Another possibility is that the agent acts out of ignorance, but they are remorseful after the fact.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s definition for what is a compulsory action. He will use this definition in order to justify many aspects of his philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>An act is involuntary if</p><ol><li>They were coerced into committing that action by an external force.</li><li>They acted out of ignorance (of the particular, not of the principle), and they repent for their actions after the fact.</li></ol><p>The agent is never morally responsible for their action if they act involuntarily.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>This is the opposite of the type of action described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>If an action is involuntary, it can never have a quality of virtuousness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are four ways of settling a belief: clinging to what one already holds, accepting what an institution imposes, adopting what is agreeable to reason or fashion, and testing against evidence that anyone can repeat.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Framing inquiry as a choice among methods rather than as a search for truth makes it possible to compare the methods by what they deliver, which is the move that grounds pragmatism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Peirce concedes that the first three are not irrational. Tenacity provides peace of mind, authority provides social stability, and the a priori method feels like reasoning. Each fails on contact with a person who has settled the same question differently, which is the pressure that pushes inquiry toward the fourth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a><ul><li>The conclusion this typology is built to support.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A belief is a habit of action\">A belief is a habit of action</a><ul><li>What is being fixed by any of the four methods.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth\">Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth</a><ul><li>A rival account of how disagreement is resolved.</li></ul></li></ul>","Censorship is bad for everyone":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should never censor someone since it has negative consequences, not only for the people censored, but for everyone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines the broad case against censorship.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two possibilities regarding the outcomes of censorship:</p><ol><li>either the orthodoxy is right, in which case divergent opinions would merely have tasted and refined the orthodox opinion, or </li><li>if the divergent opinion is true, then we have arrived closer to the truth.</li></ol><p>It seems ridiculous to claim that we are infallible, and therefore, the two previously outlines possibilities always manifest. Therefore, we must always allow for freedom of expression. By allowing freedom of expression everyone benefits from the new insights generated through the process, not just the people who otherwise would have been repressed. We can in fact produce a more accurate definition of infallibility: it is not the state of feeling sure of an opinion; rather, it is making a decision for others, and disallowing others from providing dissenting opinions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Cancel culture is a form of censorship.</li></ul></li></ul>","Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>$\\Delta$ in pH or salinity is able to break ionic bonds (see Ionic bonds transfer electrons) and hydrogen bonds (see Hydrogen bonds form between polar molecules (or charged molecules)).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may be relevant for an AP Bio test.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>A change in pH does not affect Van der Waals forces occur between all molecules .</p><h4>References</h4>","Disregarding advice grounded in others’ experience is the characteristic failure of self-reliance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The self-reliant person’s distinctive error is not overestimating their own capacity but discounting testimony, since the knowledge that would have saved them exists and is offered, and is refused because of its source.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This separates a defensible independence of judgment from the disposition that makes it fatal, which matters because the two are usually praised together.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>To Build A Fire</em> the protagonist has been told by an old-timer not to travel alone below a certain temperature, and the advice is both correct and free. He rejects it as the caution of an old man.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a><ul><li>The broader claim about solitude that this specifies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a><ul><li>The source of the testimony being discounted.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time\">Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time</a><ul><li>The capacity that refusing testimony forfeits.</li></ul></li></ul>","Equity is the rectification of legal justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Equity is defined as the rectification of legal justice, meaning the application of the generality of laws to specific circumstances.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Equity is needed to ensure that laws functions smoothly and are able to be used even when circumstances may differ slightly from the one specified in a code of law.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Equity is a kind of justice, and it is better than legal justice (since it rectifies the flaws existent in legal justice), though not as good as absolute justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a> <ul><li>Equity concerns legal justice, which is a subcategory of justice under political justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Everyone in the world should love all other people equally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the core tenant of Mohism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This principle was proven by <em>Mozi</em> with his “tests of judgement,” i.e., every principle must be judged based on its:</p><ol><li>basis,</li><li>verifiability, and</li><li>applicability.</li></ol><p>Regarding the first, <em>Mozi</em> claims that the principle is endorsed by the gods and the spirits. Regarding the second, he claims that the principle is able to be verified through the senses of the common people. Regarding the third, he claims that the principle has been applied, and it works in practice. Therefore, he believes his principle to be correct. The principle is desirable since its application guarantees the greatest amount of happiness for the greatest amount of people (this is very similar to utilitarian arguments)</p><h4>Note</h4><p>This principle is not something that is innate to humans, but rather needs to be imposed from the outside, via an absolutist monarch.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society\">Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society</a><ul><li>Provides context for which the principles discussed here were derived.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a><ul><li>This is required for the principle outlined here to be put into practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>Many of the critiques of utilitarianism could be directed here?</li></ul></li></ul>","For the sage, emotions are external":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the sage, one’s emotions are something that is external to oneself. As a result, a sage may experience emotion, though such an emotion is something external that enters us, and then passes us.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Neo-Confucianist view (specifically, the view explicated by Cheng Hao) of emotions.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The mind is a mirror for the sage, and simply reflects what is in the world. Therefore, when anger occurs it is merely reflected in the sage, and it not truly a part of him.</p><h4>References</h4>","In proteins, secondary structure describes interactions between backbones, and tertiary structure, R groups":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a protein, secondary structure is specific referring to the interactions between the backbones of a polypeptide chain. Tertiary structure refers to the interactions between the R groups of a polypeptide chain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein\">Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group\">When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group</a></li></ul>","It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This was an idea expressed by a speaker in one of Zossima’s stories. In essence, it argues that while it may be difficult to make grand propositions of one’s love of humanity in general (even being willing to sacrifice oneself for humanity), it seems much more difficult to actually love particular people, because the annoyance of mundane existence overpowers this more noble sense of universal love.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This highlights a principle that is often neglected when we think about what it means to “love” humanity. The difficulty comes down to loving individual people, not the abstract idea of “humanity.”</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Father Zossima, from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></p><p>\"It's just the same story as a doctor once told me,\" observed the elder. \"He was a man getting on in years, and undoubtedly clever. He spoke as frankiy as you, though in jest, in bitter jest. 'I love humanity,' he said, 'but I wonder at myself. <strong>The more I love humanity in general, the less I love man in particular.</strong> <strong>In my dreams,</strong>' he said, '<strong>I have often come to making enthusiastic schemes for the service of humanity, and perhaps I might actually have faced crucifixion if it had been suddenly necessary; and yet I am incapable of living in the same room with any one for two days together, as I know by experience.</strong> As soon as any one is near me, his personality disturbs my self- complacency and restricts my freedom. In twenty-four hours I begin to hate the best of men: one because he's too long over his dinner; another because he has a cold and keeps on blowing his nose. I become hostile to people the moment they come close to me. <strong>But it has always happened that the more I detest men individually the more ardent becomes my love for humanity.</strong>'\"</p></blockquote><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This seems to connect to the overall idea in <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em> of the contrast between the base and the noble. It is an example of when the base (the annoyance with others in the mundanity of life) overpowers the noble (an idealistic desire to love humanity universally). Father Zossima tries to do both, taking responsibility for all of humanity’s sins while seeking to love them individually. Ivan is almost like the man that is talked about in this passage. He has a penchant for viewing things through a spectator point of view, gaining enjoyment out of that, but when viewing things in particular, he begins to despise humanity. This is what drives his argument in “Rebellion.” We have these romanticized notions about the character traits that humanity has with regards to the good (love, compassion, etc.), though this image breaks down when we view individuals in particular.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him\">Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him</a><ul><li>This is the argument that Ivan makes in “Rebellion.”</li></ul></li></ul>","Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A common tactic used by leaders to maintain control when there is internal division is to turn your attention outwards.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one possible motivation for leaders to seek external conflict, and may explain the underlying cause of some conflicts.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.bing.com/ck/a?!&amp;&amp;p=e9adc0cd945de2565d39f8b76f7f46bea866252ed164dfc404f27d1a9a20d612JmltdHM9MTc1NzYzNTIwMA&amp;ptn=3&amp;ver=2&amp;hsh=4&amp;fclid=02b3cf74-2a65-6907-1060-d9392b216882&amp;psq=charles+x+invasion+of+algeria&amp;u=a1aHR0cHM6Ly9lbi53aWtpcGVkaWEub3JnL3dpa2kvSW52YXNpb25fb2ZfQWxnaWVyc18oMTgzMCk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Invasion of Algiers (1830)</a>: Charles X invaded Algiers in order to distract from domestic problems and restore his prestige.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Falklands_War\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Falklands War (1982)</a>: Argentina’s government ordered an invasion of the Falklands in order to distract form their popularity and mismanagement of the nation.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Russo-Japanese_War\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905)</a>: Russia attempted to attack Japan in order to distract from its internal problems. However, they were defeated.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Xunzi argues that morality exists in order for individual satisfactions to be apportioned among individuals.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Xunzi’s justification for the existence of morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since people do not all desire different things equally, and the things that people do desire are not infinitely plentiful, and because the world is limited in size, <em>li</em> (礼), which are moral guidelines for individual action, must be established in order to set limits on how much individuals can satisfy their desires; otherwise, there is conflict and war. In this way, he departs from the Confucian orthodoxy, since he claims that morality is not <em>sui generis</em>, since it could have been otherwise, if this “otherwise” led to the same final result.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>Both this linked note and the principle described in the current note are the results of a type of rough utilitarian calculus.</li></ul></li></ul>","Obstinacy is different from continence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Obstinacy is not the same thing as continence, though they may share some similarities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Continence is an important moral state, and it is important to not get it mixed up with obstinacy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Obstinacy can mean that someone is “hard to persuade” or “not easily persuaded.”<ul><li>In the former case, it means that they are difficult to change their mind. </li><li>In the latter case, it means that it is difficult to change their minds once they are already set on something.</li></ul></li><li>There are three types of obstinacy:<ul><li><strong>Opinionated</strong>: relates to pleasure and pain; such a person like being right too much. It causes them pain when they are wrong.</li><li><strong>Stupid</strong>: such people are not able to figure something out because they can’t think rationally about a problem, or they don’t have the relevant knowledge.</li><li><strong>Boorish</strong>: such people are stubborn due to lack of sensitivity or social awarness.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>Continence is different from obstinacy.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should act according to one’s natural abilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A development of the Taoist doctrine of <em>wu-wei</em> states that people should not simply “not act,” but rather that they should act according to their natural abilities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a development of Taoist theory, specifically, of the concept of <em>wu-wei</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The core issue, as identified by Guo Xiang, is that of imitation; i.e., attempting to imitate the sages and accomplish great things when it is not in one’s fundamental nature to do so. Guo rather claims that one should do what is inherently in one’s nature. Imitation, Guo claims, is useless, fruitless, and harmful.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to be useless\">One ought to be useless</a><ul><li>This note represents a development of this Taoist concept.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Guo redefines knowledge as that which is beyond one’s natural ability.</li></ul></li></ul>","Portrait of Jerónimo de Cevallos":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>I’m starting to think that El Greco is one of the most underrated portraitists of all time?</p><h4>References</h4>","Prediction markets have a tendency for longshot bias":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Prediction markets have a tendency, especially in high-engagement categories like sports and entertainment, to overestimate the odds of low probability events.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This acts as a counter against the existence of an efficient market in predicts markets, and a highlights a potential opportunity for arbitrage.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>All contracts priced below 20 cents underperform their odds, while those above 80 outperform.</li><li>This phenomenon is most observable in specific categories:</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Taker Return</th><th>Maker Return</th><th>Gap</th><th>N trades</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>World Events</td><td>-3.66%</td><td>+3.66%</td><td>7.32 pp</td><td>0.2M</td></tr><tr><td>Media</td><td>-3.64%</td><td>+3.64%</td><td>7.28 pp</td><td>0.6M</td></tr><tr><td>Entertainment</td><td>-2.40%</td><td>+2.40%</td><td>4.79 pp</td><td>1.5M</td></tr><tr><td>Crypto</td><td>-1.34%</td><td>+1.34%</td><td>2.69 pp</td><td>6.7M</td></tr><tr><td>Weather</td><td>-1.29%</td><td>+1.29%</td><td>2.57 pp</td><td>4.4M</td></tr><tr><td>Sports</td><td>-1.11%</td><td>+1.12%</td><td>2.23 pp</td><td>43.6M</td></tr><tr><td>Politics</td><td>-0.51%</td><td>+0.51%</td><td>1.02 pp</td><td>4.9M</td></tr><tr><td>Finance</td><td>-0.08%</td><td>+0.08%</td><td>0.17 pp</td><td>4.4M</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>There is a consistent advantage for NO contracts by makers (people who set limit deals and provide liquidity for the market).</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">NO contracts are better than YES contracts</p><p>Despite the market being zero-sum, dollar-weighted returns are -1.02% for YES buyers compared to +0.83% for NO buyers, a 1.85 percentage point gap driven by the overpricing of YES contracts.</p></blockquote><p></p><h4>Note</h4><p>Mostly yes. Calibrated prices are the result of makers absorbing biased flow, and the transfer turned against takers just as Susquehanna began making markets on Kalshi in 2024, so maker profits are likely competed down toward cost. The YES/NO gap is statistically fragile and small enough for fees to erase, so it is probably not a usable strategy. It likely survives only in thin categories like Media and World Events, which are too small to be worth a professional's time.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies\">The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies</a><ul><li>This note describes something that the EMH cannot account for, even outside of market anomalies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Still Life with Fish and Turtle":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>The rendering of the animals is extremely life-like and attractive, and the treatment of the squids in the bottom right is highly compelling.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Still Life, Tea Service\">Still Life, Tea Service</a><ul><li>Two vastly different styles of still life, though both are excellent.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since the content that they were testing for in the exams were largely outdated (based on Neo-Confucian teachings that were millennia-old at this point) and also that they were not an effective means of bettering the citizenry.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The abolishment of the civil service exams and the failure to erect an institution to replace it that served similar functions was cited as one of the reasons for the fall of the Qing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The civil service exams were:</p><ul><li>largely still testing the Four Books and Five Classics, and were focused on classical scholarship, rather than modern technical material, and </li><li>focused merely on producing an elite, rather than primary education, which other successful countries at the time had implemented.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The exams outlived the doctrine that justified them.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>Abolition removed the ladder that produced this class.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>This note argues that, specifically, near its end, it was not really, or not really effectively.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Outlines the true purpose of the civil service exam system.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Soviet Union left an heritage of institutional corruption and state control":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The collapse of the USSR left a lasting mark on the institutions of Russia, since elements of statism and corruption continued in the country. This is continuing to have an adverse effect on the country, politically and economically, today.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This continues to effect the functioning of the Russian state, and explains, perhaps partly, why the USSR fell back to into autocracy soon after leaving it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a><ul><li>Russia was not successful in this third wave of democratization perhaps due to their institutional heritage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This is the case in Russia.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>But sometimes they can coincide/influence/change each other?</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US betrayed its civilizational allies through the Bosnian War due to American feelings of idealism, moralism, humanitarianism, naivete, and ignorance towards the Balkans.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the US betrayed its civilizational allies during the Bosnian War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due to the ability of the Bosnian Muslims to present themselves as victims, they were supported by the US, despite their commitment of war crimes to a similar degree as Bosnian Croats and Serbs.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre\">NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre</a><ul><li>The proximate cause this note downplays. Srebrenica is the strongest case that the intervention was a response to events rather than to ideology.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Yan'an Rectification Movement solidifies Maoism within the CCP":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Yan'an Rectification Movement, carried out during the Second Sino-Japanese war, solidified Mao’s position in the CCP, and maintained ideological unity in the party. It marked a split with Soviet Marxist thought at the time, and established a cult of personality for Mao.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is when Mao’s cult of personality was established, and when the CCP officially had an ideological split with the USSR.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Maoism advocates for a &#x27;bottom-up&#x27; revolution, while Leninism argues for a &#x27;top-down&#x27; revolution\">Maoism advocates for a 'bottom-up' revolution, while Leninism argues for a 'top-down' revolution</a><ul><li>This is a crucial difference between Maoism and traditional Marxist-Leninism practiced in the USSR.</li></ul></li></ul>","The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While many point to the problem of incentives as the biggest problem facing systems like communism, these problems can usually be solved with coercion and violence. The bigger problem that is unsolvable in a non-market system is the problem of economic calculation, as since state-run economies lack market signals, their capital allocation is extremely inefficient.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the greatest problem that faces a state-run economy, important for when analyzing such economies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The common explanation for why socialist economies don’t work is that they have an incentive problem, that without proper compensation, people lack the incentives to do stuff like in a capitalist economy. While this is certainly part of the problem, the bigger problem is that of economic calculation. Without freedom to allocate capital freely as individuals, capital will not be allocated to their most productive uses, meaning that the system will not be efficient.</p><h4>Capital Markets</h4><p>A solution that is sometimes proposed is to introduce a market mechanism for consumer goods such that people are able to bid on those goods, but nationalize the means of production. This does not fundamentally solve the economic calculation problem, however, since here is no way to determine the allocatively efficient use of capital when capital is centralized. Without prices, the marginal value of heterogeneous capital cannot be found, and thus, economic calculation cannot be performed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental claim behind the Economics Calculation problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Hayek makes a less absolute claim than the conceptual problem of economic calculation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism cannot deal with change\">Socialism cannot deal with change</a><ul><li>A secondary problem with socialism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Unlike what might seem logical at first, the capitalist spirit was developed to a far greater extent in the heavily protestant North of the US, rather than the South, many of which were founded with the sole purpose of generating income.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explain the importance of religion (specifically Protestantism) when it comes to the development of the capitalist spirit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a><ul><li>The Calvinist doctrine that the North carried and the South did not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Explains the puzzle: the commercial South was more traditionalist despite being more commercial.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism\">The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism</a></li></ul>","The liberal nation state is doomed to fail since it gives people nothing to preserve":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The liberal state, since it is ahistorical and bureaucratic, is weak and doomed to fail, especially in conflict against a civilizational state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Liberal states don’t have the same stakes that civilizational states have, and therefore, they are weaker when fighting against civilizational states.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The liberal nation-state has nothing to defend, since by its very nature, it serves only as a bureaucratic supplier of goods and services, which almost never gives its clients value for money. On the other hand, it demands that citizens sacrifice their lives for the state. </li><li>This can be analogized to “dying for the telephone company.”</li><li>Liberalism does not provide a hierarchy of goods as a civilization does, and it is supremely ahistorical.</li><li>A civilizational state seeks defense, but not the conquest of other territories. On the other hand, the liberal state has an inherently imperialistic impulse as it seeks to level the hierarchy of goods which civilizational state possess and replace it with a bureaucracy.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order\">The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order</a><ul><li>This book offers a similar perspective.</li></ul></li></ul>","The princes of Russia attempted to ward the Mongol threat off alone, and were defeated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because the princes of Russia refused to cooperate each other, they were individually easily defeated by the invading Mongols.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates one of the major advantages that the Mongols had over their enemies. The Mongols were an united, overwhelming force, while their enemies were often weak and divided.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a></li></ul>","The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Scientific knowledge (<em>episteme</em>) deals with necessary/eternal truths and uses logic (deductive reasoning, syllogisms, etc.) in order to get from the first principles to the conclusion. This virtue is part of the scientific soul.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important virtue that makes up one of the parts of wisdom.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a><ul><li>Supplies the first principles that this virtue reasons from. Deduction cannot reach its own premises.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a><ul><li>Scientific knowledge forms a part of wisdom.</li></ul></li></ul>","There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a token undergoes inference, there is a limited amount of compute that can be applied to each input token.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that reasoning should be spread across various tokens (and thus compute) in order to generate the best response.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For example, asking an LLM to generate the response to a math question and asking it to do by only providing the answer and not the steps crams all of the calculations into one token, thus lowering the potential quality of the response.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window\">To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window</a><ul><li>A practical consequence of this constraint.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"LLM hallucinations are &#x27;shameless guesses,&#x27; not &#x27;hallucinations&#x27;\">LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'</a><ul><li>When LLMs make mistakes due to the fact that they were provided too little tokens for a response, this could be the justification.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT\">Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT</a><ul><li>Reasoning LLMs could operate in a different manner, where this is less of a problem?</li></ul></li></ul>","When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When discussing the polarity of an amino acid, what is in fact being discussed is the polarity of the R group, not of the backbone. The backbone is always polar.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li>The more polar a molecule, the more soluble it is in water<ul><li>This explains further the implication of the property of polarity that is described in this note.</li></ul></li><li>Hydrogen bonds form between polar molecules (or charged molecules)<ul><li>A type of bond that is formed between the subject of this note, polar molecules.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein\">Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein</a><ul><li>A related note that describes the nature of non-polar notes.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
